 functioning we want to keep in mind  these four main areas so are they  engaged in what they're learning are  they seeing friends do they seem  connected to you  uh these are the things that can guide  us when we're not so sure  so how do you know dr howard if there's  a problem because i think seven to eight  times out of ten if you say to your  child how are you doing they'll say fine  right everything's fine or they don't  want to talk to you about it whatsoever  so how do you break through to start a  conversation and what should you be  looking out for you've touched on it  briefly  yeah so i think what we want to do is is  create  opportunities to discuss this keep an  open line of communication a lot of  times we don't have great vocabulary for  talking about our emotions and our  behaviors and so the more that you can  bring the conversation up and normalize  it and not necessarily jump to  a dramatic kind of conversation kids  hate that especially teenagers so the  more that you can sort of they chill and  continue to engage them um the more  likely they will wait jamie jamie what  did you call that fake chill is that  what you called it fake chill you yeah  well you want to be chill try and fake  it till you make it even if you have to  that's okay  yeah yeah yeah jamie um i was once told  when it comes to my own mental health  treat it like mask falling from above on  a turbulent flight in other words  assist yourself before you assist your  child  so for  parents or caregivers that might be  dealing with their own mental health  issues what advice do you give them  before they go and approach their own  kids  absolutely there's so there's so much  importance there because you're modeling  taking care of yourself and you're  destigmatizing mental health care  by getting treatment yourself so if you  can sort of vocalize that and say you  know what i've been feeling really  anxious with the omicron variants now  spreading across the country something  i'm